# GNOME desktop (SDD §11). Bluefin-like UX, independently maintained.

gnome-shell

# Rule 11: provisioning that needs a user account (group membership, Homebrew)
# can only run AFTER gnome-initial-setup creates it — i.e. once the user is
# already looking at their desktop. The boot splash cannot cover that phase, so
# without a notification the machine silently downloads for several minutes and
# looks broken. Used by ik-os-homebrew and ik-os-user-groups.
libnotify-bin
# The progress window ik-os-provisioning-monitor draws while Homebrew and the
# company Brewfile install after first login. zenity's stdin protocol means no
# GTK code of ours; measured against the image's dpkg database this and
# zenity-common are the only two packages it adds.
zenity
gnome-session
gdm3
gnome-initial-setup             # SDD §36/§37 — creates the first user account on
                                # first boot. The image ships no accounts and a
                                # locked root (SDD §27), so without this GDM comes
                                # up with nothing to log in as.
gnome-control-center            # printer UI required by SDD §23
gnome-keyring                   # the Secret Service. Nothing else in this
                                # image provides org.freedesktop.secrets, so
                                # without it there is no store for Wi-Fi
                                # passwords, the VPN password (which ADR 0018
                                # deliberately does not keep in the profile),
                                # or anything else using libsecret --
                                # libsecret-1-0 is the client half and was
                                # already here with no server to talk to.
libpam-gnome-keyring            # unlocks that keyring with the login password.
                                # Without it the user is asked for a second
                                # password at every login, which is the usual
                                # way people end up storing nothing in it.
# gnome-control-center *Recommends* this, and the build installs with
# --no-install-recommends, so it was silently absent. It is the GTK3 editor for
# the ik-office VPN profile: Settings' own panel edits only name and IP, while
# this one reaches the OpenVPN fields (remote, cipher, certificate paths) that
# config/company/vpn/ik-office.nmconnection.in sets. Debian forky split it out
# of the old network-manager-gnome, which no longer exists as a package -- the
# applet half is now network-manager-applet, which GNOME Shell does not need.
nm-connection-editor
gnome-settings-daemon
gnome-terminal
nautilus
gnome-text-editor
gnome-disk-utility
# No gnome-system-monitor either: Mission Center replaces it as a Flatpak.
# Its two privileged helpers do have to live here, though: a sandboxed app
# cannot give a binary file capabilities, and cannot write them to a read-only
# /usr afterwards (docs/adr/0013-mission-center-host-helpers.md).
nethogs                         # per-process bandwidth; needs file capabilities
lm-sensors                      # sensors-detect, which the app's setup probes for
# No gnome-software. The graphical app store is Bazaar, installed as a Flatpak
# (config/desktop/system-flatpaks.list) — a Flatpak-native store for a
# Flatpak-first desktop (SDD §15, §54), and one less GUI stack in the immutable
# image.
xdg-desktop-portal
xdg-desktop-portal-gnome
xdg-user-dirs-gtk
dconf-cli                       # required to compile desktop/gnome/dconf at build time
libglib2.0-bin                  # gsettings, used to validate the dconf keys at build time
plymouth
plymouth-themes                 # branding watermark (branding/plymouth)
fonts-cantarell
pipewire
pipewire-pulse
wireplumber
power-profiles-daemon
gnome-shell-extension-manager   # lets developers manage their own extensions (SDD §53)

# Anthropic's first-party package, from their own signed APT repository
# (config/apt/claude-desktop.sources). Not in Debian and not on Flathub, so
# SDD §54's Flatpak route does not apply — see docs/adr/0007-claude-desktop.md.
claude-desktop

# Electron runtime dependencies, shared by the three applications this image
# unpacks from upstream .debs rather than installing: draw.io (ADR 0016), Sidra
# (ADR 0019) and teams-for-linux (ADR 0020). All three are electron-builder
# packages and all three declare exactly this Depends field, which apt never
# reads because the payload is unpacked rather than installed — so it is
# transcribed here. Each of 52-drawio.sh, 53-sidra.sh and 54-teams.sh runs ldd
# over its own unpacked binaries and fails the build if this list is incomplete.
#
# Two names differ from what upstream declares, because forky's 64-bit time_t
# transition renamed them and the originals no longer exist in the suite:
# libgtk-3-0 -> libgtk-3-0t64, libatspi2.0-0 -> libatspi2.0-0t64. A third,
# libappindicator3-1, was dropped from Debian entirely; libayatana-* is the
# maintained fork.
libgtk-3-0t64
libnotify4
libnss3
libxss1
libxtst6
libatspi2.0-0t64
libuuid1
libsecret-1-0
xdg-utils
libayatana-appindicator3-1      # Recommends: the tray icon
#
# NOT in upstream's Depends, and found only by the ldd check in 52-drawio.sh:
# the Electron binary links both directly. Installing the .deb with apt would
# have "succeeded" and the app would have failed to launch.
libgbm1                         # libgbm.so.1
libasound2t64                   # libasound.so.2 (t64-renamed, like the two above)
#
# DevPod (ADR 0021) is not an Electron app -- it is Tauri, so it renders through
# the system WebKit rather than a bundled Chromium. Its other two Depends
# (libgtk-3-0 -> libgtk-3-0t64, libayatana-appindicator3-1) are already above;
# this is the one package in the image that exists for DevPod alone. Note the
# image already has WebKit for GTK4 (libwebkitgtk-6.0-4, via GNOME): this is a
# second, older build of the same engine, ~126 MB installed with its
# JavaScriptCore. Both are Debian's and both get Debian's security updates,
# which is why DevPod's renderer ages better than the Electron three.
libwebkit2gtk-4.1-0             # the GTK3 WebKit; DevPod Desktop is a Tauri app
shared-mime-info                # update-mime-database, so .drawio files get a type
desktop-file-utils              # update-desktop-database, so the launcher is indexed
